At this stage, we start shaping the raw crime and weather data into something more insightful. Instead of just looking at raw numbers, we add useful context—like which day of the week an incident happened, whether it fell on a weekend or public holiday, and which season it occurred in, based on UK weather patterns.We also introduce new weather-related features, such as whether it rained on a particular day or how comfortable the temperature was. To better understand crime locations while keeping privacy in mind, we slightly round off the coordinates, which helps us detect neighborhood-level hotspots without revealing exact addresses.

These new features add depth to the data, helping us capture patterns related to time, place, and environment. They’re especially valuable for creating meaningful visualizations and building predictive models later on.

# Crime Pattern Analysis

## Comprehensive Crime Statistics

```{r crime-statistics}
# Calculate detailed crime statistics
crime_stats <- crime_data %>%
  group_by(category_clean) %>%
  summarise(
    Total = n(),
    Percentage = round(n() / nrow(crime_data) * 100, 2),
    Weekend_Count = sum(is_weekend),
    Weekend_Pct = round(sum(is_weekend) / n() * 100, 1),
    Summer_Count = sum(season == "Summer"),
    Summer_Pct = round(sum(season == "Summer") / n() * 100, 1),
    .groups = 'drop'
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(Total)) %>%
  mutate(
    Rank = row_number(),
    Cumulative_Pct = cumsum(Percentage),
    Icon = case_when(
      Rank <= 3 ~ "🔴",
      Rank <= 6 ~ "🟡",
