    }
  }

  # Render combined subplot layout
  subplot(subplot_list, nrows = 1, shareY = FALSE, titleX = TRUE, margin = 0.05) %>%
    layout(
      title = list(
        text = "<b>Crime Types vs Temperature (Monthly)</b><br><span style='color:gray;'>Blue = Rainy Month, Orange = Dry Month</span>",
        font = list(size = 20),
        y = 0.95,
        x = 0.5,
        xanchor = "center",
        yanchor = "top"
      ),
      margin = list(t = 120, l = 60, r = 40, b = 60)
    )
} else {
  htmltools::div(style = "color: red; font-weight: bold;",
                 "⚠ Cannot render scatter plots — required variables missing or insufficient data.")
}
```
Analyzing monthly data reveals a noticeable pattern between temperature fluctuations and crime levels in Colchester. Warmer months typically correspond with higher crime rates, especially for violent and property-related offenses. The bubble sizes in the scatter plot represent average rainfall, indicating that increased precipitation often correlates with reduced criminal activity. These insights suggest that public safety efforts should be seasonally adjusted to reflect environmental influences.


