  # Plot Observed vs Trend
  plot_ly(monthly_data, x = ~month) %>%
    add_lines(y = ~total_crimes, name = "Observed", line = list(color = "#2C3E50")) %>%
    add_lines(y = ~trend, name = "LOESS Trend", line = list(color = "#E74C3C", dash = "dash")) %>%
    layout(
      title = "<b>Monthly Crime Trend (LOESS)</b><br><sup style='color:gray;'>Smoothed crime pattern using loess</sup>",
      yaxis = list(title = "Crime Count"),
      xaxis = list(title = "Month"),
      hovermode = "x unified"
    )
} else {
  htmltools::div(style = "color: red;", "⚠ Not enough monthly data to show trend.")
}
```
When looking at monthly crime data, it can be tough to spot real trends because of natural ups and downs. To help with that, we use a smoothing method called LOESS that draws a gentle curve through the data. This makes it easier to see whether crime has been going up, down, or staying steady over time.

By placing this trendline next to the actual monthly numbers, we can clearly spot any long-term changes. These shifts might be linked to things like seasonal changes, local events, or even new policing strategies. It’s a helpful way to keep track of how things are evolving and whether any actions are making a difference.


