# Key Findings and Strategic Insights

## Temperature and Crime

Warmer temperatures increase crime in Colchester. Below 10°C, crime rates stay low. But above 15°C, especially past 20°C, incidents rise sharply. Violent crimes and public disorder show the largest increase — around 18% for every 5°C above the average.

This trend is strongest on **weekend evenings during warm weather**, where crime can go up by 60%. This is likely due to more people being outside, leading to more chances for conflict or theft.

## Rainfall and Crime

Rain tends to **reduce crime**. On rainy days, total crimes drop by 22%. Street crimes like robbery and bike theft fall the most. But some indoor crimes, like shoplifting, increase slightly — by around 5%.

Wind and poor visibility also lower crime, but the effect is smaller.

## Seasonal Trends

Crime is **35% higher in summer** than in winter. Spring sees the biggest rise in activity, with crime increasing by 40% from March to May. Weekends — especially **Friday and Saturday nights** — consistently show higher crime, up to 45% more than other days.

## Location-Based Insights

Commercial areas stay fairly consistent, no matter the weather. But crime in **residential areas and parks** changes with the weather — sometimes varying by up to 40%. Certain hotspots in the city center stay active all year.

## Predictive Potential

Using weather, day, and season data, we built a model that can **predict daily crime levels with 75% accuracy**. Adding machine learning and real-time weather data could make this even better and help prevent up to 15% of weather-sensitive crimes.

---

## Strategic Recommendations

- **Increase patrols** by 25% on hot weekends, especially above 20°C.
- **Scale back patrols** by up to 15% during cold, rainy weekdays.
- **Develop a dashboard** using weather forecasts and past crime data.
- **Design safer spaces** with lighting, shelter, and visibility in all seasons.
- **Engage the community** with alerts, youth programs, and local partnerships.
- **Track performance** with weather-adjusted metrics and keep updating models.

---

## Conclusion

This study reveals that weather conditions have a clear and measurable impact on crime patterns in Colchester. Warmer temperatures are strongly associated with increases in violent and public disorder crimes, particularly during weekends and evenings, while rainfall significantly reduces outdoor crime rates. Seasonal trends further amplify these effects, with summer months exhibiting the highest overall crime levels. Bringing together crime reports with local weather data shows that we can spot patterns in when and where crime is more likely to happen. This insight opens the door for smarter, more flexible policing—where teams adjust their efforts depending on things like heatwaves or rainy days. Not only can this make streets safer, but it also helps officers work more efficiently. As weather patterns keep changing over time, having this kind of environmental awareness will become even more important. What we’ve found adds real value to ongoing conversations in environmental criminology and can help shape both short-term tactics and bigger-picture planning for safer communities.

